Chapter: E1000e
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter MemoryMap
  cites Kernel chapter Hpet
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 Intel gigabit Ethernet, the 8254x/8257x/I21x descriptor-ring family.

Section: Identification

  e1000-vendor-intel : Integer = #8086
  e1000-class-network : Integer = 2
  e1000-subclass-ethernet : Integer = 0

  e1000-find : PciScanResult -> Maybe PciDevice
  e1000-find (scan) = e1000-find-loop (scan.devices) 0 (scan.count)

  e1000-find-loop : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer -> Maybe PciDevice
  e1000-find-loop (devs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then None
   else let d = list-at devs i
   in if e1000-is-candidate d then Just d
   else e1000-find-loop devs (i + 1) len

  e1000-is-candidate : PciDevice -> Boolean
  e1000-is-candidate (d) =
   if (d.pci-vendor) /= e1000-vendor-intel then False
   else if (d.pci-class) /= e1000-class-network then False
   else (d.pci-subclass) == e1000-subclass-ethernet

Section: Register Offsets

 Byte offsets into the BAR 0 memory window.

  e1000-reg-ctrl : Integer = #0000
  e1000-reg-status : Integer = #0008
  e1000-reg-ctrl-ext : Integer = #0018
  e1000-reg-icr : Integer = #00C0
  e1000-reg-imc : Integer = #00D8
  e1000-reg-rctl : Integer = #0100
  e1000-reg-tctl : Integer = #0400
  e1000-reg-tipg : Integer = #0410
  e1000-reg-rdbal : Integer = #2800
  e1000-reg-rdbah : Integer = #2804
  e1000-reg-rdlen : Integer = #2808
  e1000-reg-rdh : Integer = #2810
  e1000-reg-rdt : Integer = #2818
  e1000-reg-tdbal : Integer = #3800
  e1000-reg-tdbah : Integer = #3804
  e1000-reg-tdlen : Integer = #3808
  e1000-reg-tdh : Integer = #3810
  e1000-reg-tdt : Integer = #3818
  e1000-reg-mta : Integer = #5200
  e1000-reg-ral : Integer = #5400
  e1000-reg-rah : Integer = #5404
  e1000-reg-mdic : Integer = #0020

 The receive statistics, which are the only place a driver can ask whether a
 frame ever reached the MAC without going through its own descriptor ring.
 That distinction is the whole reason they are here: a ring showing nothing
 looks identical whether the wire was silent or the writeback landed somewhere
 the driver is not reading. These count at the part, before the ring.

 THEY CLEAR ON READ (8254x 13.4), so a second read reports the interval since
 the first and not the total. Read each one once and bank it.

  e1000-reg-crcerrs : Integer = #4000
  e1000-reg-mpc : Integer = #4010
  e1000-reg-gprc : Integer = #4074
  e1000-reg-rnbc : Integer = #40A0

  e1000-ctrl-slu : Integer = #00000040
  e1000-ctrl-asde : Integer = #00000020
  e1000-ctrl-rst : Integer = #04000000

  e1000-clear-asde : Integer = #FFFFFFFF - e1000-ctrl-asde

  e1000-status-lu : Integer = #00000002

 UPE and MPE are promiscuous unicast and multicast, set during bring-up
 deliberately: the first honest proof that this driver receives is a
 frame that was already on the wire, and a MAC filter that is subtly
 wrong looks exactly like a driver that does not receive.

  e1000-rctl-en : Integer = #00000002
  e1000-rctl-upe : Integer = #00000008
  e1000-rctl-mpe : Integer = #00000010
  e1000-rctl-bam : Integer = #00008000
  e1000-rctl-secrc : Integer = #04000000

 CT is bits 11:4 and COLD bits 21:12, so these set 15 and 64.

  e1000-tctl-en : Integer = #00000002
  e1000-tctl-psp : Integer = #00000008
  e1000-tctl-ct : Integer = #000000F0
  e1000-tctl-cold : Integer = #00040000

 IPGT at bit 0, IPGR1 at bit 10, IPGR2 at bit 20: 10, 8 and 6.

  e1000-tipg-default : Integer = #0060200A

  e1000-rx-status-dd : Integer = #01
  e1000-rx-status-eop : Integer = #02

  e1000-tx-cmd-eop : Integer = #01
  e1000-tx-cmd-ifcs : Integer = #02
  e1000-tx-cmd-rs : Integer = #08
  e1000-tx-status-dd : Integer = #01

Section: Ring Geometry

 A descriptor is sixteen bytes and the hardware requires the ring base
 to be sixteen-byte aligned and its length in bytes to be a multiple of
 128. Sixteen descriptors of sixteen bytes is 256, which satisfies both.

  e1000-desc-size : Integer = 16
  e1000-rx-count : Integer = 16
  e1000-tx-count : Integer = 16
  e1000-buf-size : Integer = 2048
  e1000-max-frame : Integer = 1522

Section: Where the BAR Is Allowed to Land

  e1000-window-lo : Integer = bare-metal-device-window-lo
  e1000-window-hi : Integer = bare-metal-device-window-hi

  e1000-bar-ok : Integer = 0
  e1000-bar-is-io : Integer = 1
  e1000-bar-below-window : Integer = 2
  e1000-bar-above-window : Integer = 3
  e1000-bar-zero : Integer = 4

  e1000-bar-verdict : PciBar -> Integer
  e1000-bar-verdict (bar) =
   if (bar.bar-is-io) then e1000-bar-is-io
   else if (bar.bar-base) == 0 then e1000-bar-zero
   else if (bar.bar-base) >= e1000-window-hi then e1000-bar-above-window
   else if (bar.bar-base) < e1000-window-lo then e1000-bar-below-window
   else e1000-bar-ok

Section: Device Handle

  E1000Device = record {
    e-mmio : Integer,
    e-rx-ring : Integer,
    e-tx-ring : Integer,
    e-rx-bufs : Integer,
    e-tx-bufs : Integer,
    e-ctrl-blk : Integer,
    e-present : Boolean,
    e-mac : List Integer,
    e-mac-valid : Boolean,
    e-pch : Integer,
    e-ulp : Integer
  }

 `e-ulp` CARRIES `e1000-ulp-step`'S ANSWER for the same reason `e-pch` carries
 `e1000-pch-prepare`'S: a step whose result nothing reads is a step nothing can
 report on, which is the defect that stood in this chapter until 2026-08-21.
 0 is "not asked or not a PCH part", 1 is "the entry bits are clear", 2 is "the
 write did not take", -1 is "the register did not answer".

 `e-pch` CARRIES `e1000-pch-prepare`'S ANSWER OFF THE ONE PATH THAT RUNS IT.
 Every other construction site names it 0, which is the answer's own word for
 "not an I219, the step did not run", and that is true of all of them: the
 diag stages build a device by hand to read registers and never run the step.
 CDX2006 refuses a literal that leaves the field out, so there is no site
 where this reads as zero by accident rather than by statement.

  e1000-cb-rx-next : Integer = 0
  e1000-cb-tx-next : Integer = 4
  e1000-cb-size : Integer = 16

Section: Register Access

 Identity paging makes a virtual address its own physical address, so a
 BAR value is usable as written and a buffer address is usable as a DMA
 target without translation.

  e1000-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-read (mmio) (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (mmio + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  e1000-write : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-write (mmio) (off) (v) = poke-32 (mmio + off) 0 v

Section: Memory

 alloc-bytes is a bump of the heap pointer and does not zero what it
 returns, so every descriptor field this driver relies on is written
 explicitly. It also gives no alignment guarantee, so a ring over-
 allocates by one alignment unit and rounds the base up.

  e1000-align : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-align (addr) (n) =
   let r = int-mod addr n
   in if r == 0 then addr else addr + (n - r)

  e1000-alloc-aligned : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-alloc-aligned (bytes) (align) =
   let raw = alloc-bytes (bytes + align)
   in e1000-align raw align

  e1000-zero-words : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-zero-words (base) (count) (i) =
   if i >= count then 0
   else let w = poke-32 (base + i * 4) 0 0
   in e1000-zero-words base count (i + 1)

Section: Reset and Link

 The reset sequence the 8254x datasheet asks for: mask every interrupt so a
 half-configured device cannot raise one, pulse RST, wait for the bit to
 clear itself, then mask again because the reset restores the interrupt
 mask to its power-on state.

 WHICH DATASHEET MATTERS AND THIS LINE USED TO SAY "THE" DATASHEET. Measured
 2026-08-21 against `Intel_I219_Datasheet.txt` section 5.2, "Reset Operation":
 the I219's reset sources are FOUR and a MAC software reset is not among them.
 They are the internal power-on reset, the PHY soft reset (MDIO register 0 bit
 15), the PCIe reset pin, and an in-band reset message. `CTRL.RST` is an
 8254x/8257x MAC register bit, and on this part the MAC is not a discrete
 controller: it sits in the PCH, whose CSR map is in no document we hold. So
 the sequence above is CORROBORATED BY FAMILY, NOT CITED FOR THE PART, exactly
 as `EXTCNF_CTRL` is, and there is NO I219 citation requiring the receiver to
 be quiesced before it -- nor one permitting it. The 8254x's own order does
 disable RX and TX first, which is the whole basis for the convention below.

 IT DOES NOT QUIESCE, AND THE CALLER MUST. This pulses RST without touching
 RCTL or TCTL, so calling it on a part whose receiver is RUNNING pulses reset
 with descriptors in flight. Call `e1000-quiesce` first if anything has
 brought the part up since power-on. `e1000-init` is safe because it resets a
 part before it configures one; every OTHER caller is responsible, and the
 convention is invisible from here, which is what makes it a trap.

 WHAT IT COST, 2026-08-20: the diagnostic ladder's ASDE stage wedged the ASUS
 doing exactly this. `nicinit` and `b3` had each run `e1000-init` and
 `nicring` had built live rings, so by the time the stage reset the part the
 receiver was enabled, and the glass sat on that step for five minutes.
 **The evidence is the BOUND rather than the story**: `e1000-await-reset` is
 a COUNT of a million MMIO reads, about a second on metal, so this function
 MUST return within seconds whether the bit clears or not. Five minutes means
 the reads themselves stopped returning, which is the part no longer
 answering MMIO at all. The DMA-in-flight explanation fits that and is not
 proved by it.

 A BED RUN CANNOT SPEAK TO THIS, AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
 codex-vm does not model a DMA-in-flight reset hazard, so an arm that resets a
 live receiver there completes and proves nothing about the part.
 `codex/test/e1000-reset-live` is that null, written down: under `-i219` the
 receiver is live at `rctl=67141658` -- the same value `nicring` banks on the
 board -- and `e1000-reset` answers 1; after `e1000-quiesce` leaves `rctl=0`
 it answers 1 again. `bed separates them: no`. The arm exists so nobody later
 reads a green bed run as evidence that this is safe.

 THE LOOPS ARE ALREADY ELIMINATED, by a board reading rather than by any of
 this. Sitting 8's stepped bring-up banked `s2 await-reset ret 1 in 16us`,
 because CTRL is read-only on this part: the RST bit never sets, so
 `e1000-await-reset`'s FIRST read already sees it clear and the million-read
 fuel is never approached. `e1000-settle-mdio` is bounded by its own budget
 and was measured at 10036 us in the same flight. So a hang inside this
 function is not a loop spinning; it is a SINGLE non-looping MMIO access that
 did not return, which is the part having stopped answering, and sitting 10's
 `step=reset` narrows it to these seven operations.

  e1000-reset : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-reset (mmio) =
   let m1 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
   in let ctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let r = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-or ctrl e1000-ctrl-rst)
   in let settled = e1000-await-reset mmio 0
   in let waited = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
   in let m2 = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-imc #FFFFFFFF
   in let cleared = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-icr
   in settled

  e1000-reset-fuel : Integer = 1000000

  e1000-await-reset : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-reset (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-reset-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl) e1000-ctrl-rst == 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-reset mmio (i + 1)

 Intel I219 datasheet rev 2.02 section 9.2 requires this delay between a
 reset and the first MDIO access.

  e1000-mdio-window-ms : Integer = 10

  e1000-settle-ticks-fuel : Integer = 200000
  e1000-settle-spin-fuel : Integer = 100000

  e1000-settle-mdio : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-settle-mdio (mmio) =
   let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-settle-spin mmio e1000-settle-spin-fuel
   else e1000-await-ticks hpet-ticks ((rate * e1000-mdio-window-ms) / 1000) 0

  e1000-await-ticks : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-ticks (start) (want) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-settle-ticks-fuel then 0
   else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then 1
   else e1000-await-ticks start want (i + 1)

  e1000-settle-spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-settle-spin (mmio) (i) =
   if i <= 0 then 0
   else let v = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in e1000-settle-spin mmio (i - 1)

  e1000-link-fuel : Integer = 4000000

 MDIC is the only door to the PHY. On the 8254x the PHY is a discrete part
 on an MDIO bus; on the I219 the MAC and PHY are both inside the PCH and
 this same register reaches it. Software writes MDIC with the address, the
 opcode and (for a write) the data, leaving R clear, then polls until the
 MAC sets R. E is the MAC saying the transaction failed, which is a
 different answer from a read of zero and must not be folded into one:
 nothing listening at an address reads as E, while a register genuinely
 holding zero reads as R with zero data.

  e1000-mdic-op-read : Integer = #08000000
  e1000-mdic-op-write : Integer = #04000000
  e1000-mdic-r : Integer = #10000000
  e1000-mdic-e : Integer = #40000000
  e1000-mdic-data : Integer = #0000FFFF
  e1000-mdic-fuel : Integer = 100000

 The PHY answers at one address on this family.

  e1000-phy-addr : Integer = 1
  e1000-phy-bmcr : Integer = 0
  e1000-phy-bmsr : Integer = 1
  e1000-phy-id1 : Integer = 2

  e1000-bmcr-reset : Integer = #8000
  e1000-bmcr-aneg-en : Integer = #1000
  e1000-bmcr-aneg-rst : Integer = #0200
  e1000-bmsr-aneg-done : Integer = #0020

  e1000-mdic-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-mdic-frame (op) (phy) (reg) (data) =
   bit-or op (bit-or (bit-shl phy 21)
    (bit-or (bit-shl reg 16) (bit-and data e1000-mdic-data)))

 Answers negative one for a PHY that never reports ready and for one that
 reports an error, and the caller must not treat either as a register
 value. A sixteen-bit register cannot hold negative one, so the sentinel
 cannot collide with a legitimate reading.

  e1000-phy-read-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-read-at (mmio) (phy) (reg) =
   let f = e1000-mdic-frame e1000-mdic-op-read phy reg 0
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-mdic f
   in e1000-await-mdic mmio 0

  e1000-phy-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-read (mmio) (reg) = e1000-phy-read-at mmio e1000-phy-addr reg

  e1000-phy-write : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-write (mmio) (reg) (data) =
   let f = e1000-mdic-frame e1000-mdic-op-write e1000-phy-addr reg data
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-mdic f
   in e1000-await-mdic mmio 0

  e1000-await-mdic : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-mdic (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-mdic-fuel then (0 - 1)
   else let v = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-mdic
    in if bit-and v e1000-mdic-e /= 0 then (0 - 1)
    else if bit-and v e1000-mdic-r /= 0 then bit-and v e1000-mdic-data
    else e1000-await-mdic mmio (i + 1)

 Register 31 is the page register and only its 11 high bits select, so the
 page is written shifted by five. Registers 0 to 15 are the IEEE set and
 are the same in every page, so this is needed only above 15.

  e1000-phy-page-reg : Integer = 31
  e1000-phy-port-page : Integer = 769
  e1000-phy-custom-mode : Integer = 16
  e1000-phy-mdio-slow : Integer = #0400

  e1000-phy-set-page : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-set-page (mmio) (page) =
   e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-page-reg (bit-shl page 5)

 Set after the PHY reset, never before: a reset returns the paged
 registers to their defaults and would take this with them.

  e1000-phy-slow-mode : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-slow-mode (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio e1000-phy-port-page
   in if p < 0 then 0
   else let cur = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-custom-mode
   in if cur < 0 then 0
   else let want = bit-or cur e1000-phy-mdio-slow
   in let w = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-custom-mode want
   in let back = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in if w < 0 then 0 else 1

  e1000-phy-bring-up : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-phy-bring-up (mmio) =
   let r = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-bmcr e1000-bmcr-reset
   in if r < 0 then 0
   else let s = e1000-phy-slow-mode mmio
   in let mode = bit-or e1000-bmcr-aneg-en e1000-bmcr-aneg-rst
    in let a = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-bmcr mode
    in if a < 0 then 0
    else e1000-await-aneg mmio 0

 THE FUEL WAS A COUNT AND ON REAL MDIO A COUNT IS NOT A DURATION. Measured on
 the ASUS 2026-08-15: this loop ran its full million at 92.89 us per iteration
 and returned 0 after 92,892,733 us, which was 92.9 of the 93 seconds
 `e1000-init` took and the reason the flight before it was read as a wedge.
 Every iteration is a full MDIC transaction; on a 2.5 MHz MDIO bus that is tens
 of microseconds, and in the bed the same million is free.

 The cost was diagnosed on 2026-08-04 and NOT fixed here: `NicAsde` routed
 around this function instead (see `na-phy-kick`), so the driver kept the
 million and it cost a second flight eleven days later.

 The time bound is borrowed from `e1000-settle-mdio` and `e1000-quiesce`, and
 the same flight is the evidence they work: both asked for 10 ms through HPET
 and returned in 10044 us and 10043 us on that board.

 3000 ms is a BUDGET, not a datasheet figure. Auto-negotiation on a healthy
 link completes well inside a second; three seconds is generous enough not to
 cut off a slow partner and short enough that a part which never reports done
 costs three seconds instead of ninety-three.

 THE FUEL SURVIVES as a second bound. With no HPET (rate 0) the counted path is
 the only one there is, and if HPET stops advancing mid-loop the count is what
 still ends it. Either bound fires.

 THE TIMEOUT BRANCH HAS NO BED ARM and that is a gap rather than an oversight:
 the model reports aneg done on the first read, so the loop returns 1 before a
 deadline is ever consulted. Reaching it needs an emulator flag.
 `codex/test/e1000-aneg-budget` pins the arithmetic; the behaviour rests on the
 metal measurement above.

  e1000-aneg-fuel : Integer = 1000000
  e1000-aneg-ms : Integer = 3000

  e1000-aneg-ticks : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-aneg-ticks (rate) = (rate * e1000-aneg-ms) / 1000

  e1000-await-aneg : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-aneg (mmio) (i) =
   let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-await-aneg-counted mmio i
   else e1000-await-aneg-timed mmio hpet-ticks (e1000-aneg-ticks rate) i

  e1000-await-aneg-counted : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-aneg-counted (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-aneg-fuel then 0
   else let s = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-bmsr
    in if s < 0 then 0
    else if bit-and s e1000-bmsr-aneg-done /= 0 then 1
    else e1000-await-aneg-counted mmio (i + 1)

  e1000-await-aneg-timed : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-aneg-timed (mmio) (start) (want) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-aneg-fuel then 0
   else let s = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-bmsr
    in if s < 0 then 0
    else if bit-and s e1000-bmsr-aneg-done /= 0 then 1
    else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then 0
    else e1000-await-aneg-timed mmio start want (i + 1)

 ASDE is CLEARED, and the datasheet is unusually blunt about it. 82583V
 CTRL, bit 5: "When set to 1b, the MAC ignores the speed indicated by the
 PHY and attempts to automatically detect the resolved speed of the link
 and configure itself appropriately. This bit must be set to 0b in the
 82583V." A driver that sets it is asking the MAC to disregard the one
 part that knows the answer. SLU is the opposite case, required by the
 same table: "The Set Link Up bit MUST be set to 1b to permit the MAC to
 recognize the link signal from the PHY."

  e1000-link-up : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-link-up (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-bring-up mmio
   in let ctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let w = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-and (bit-or ctrl e1000-ctrl-slu) e1000-clear-asde)
   in e1000-link-wait mmio

 ANEG DOES NOT COMPLETE ON THIS PART AND THE LINK COMES UP ANYWAY. Measured on
 the ASUS 2026-08-15: `e1000-await-aneg` ran its full million and returned 0,
 so BMSR never reported aneg-done, while `STATUS.LU` did come up and the part
 negotiated 1000 Mb/s. The two are independent readings and only the second one
 decides whether frames can move, which is why SLU is set above and this loop
 polls STATUS rather than the PHY.

 THAT MADE THIS LOOP'S MISSING BUDGET INVISIBLE FOR AS LONG AS ANEG WAS SLOW.
 The million-iteration aneg wait was 92.9 seconds of dead time during which the
 link came up behind it, so by the time this ran the answer was already yes and
 the count was never approached. Budgeting aneg at 3 s removed the dead time and
 this loop then ran its full four million against a part whose link was still
 settling: measured 2026-08-16, over ten minutes with no return, which reads as
 a wedge. The regression was mine (15463); the defect is older and was masked.

 AND THAT SENTENCE IS NO LONGER THE LAST WORD ON A LONG WAIT HERE, because
 sitting 9 ruled this loop out for the hang it describes. Stage 12 banked
 `s9 phy-bring-up ret=0 us=3000419` and `s10 await-link ret=1 us=36` on the
 ASUS: the aneg budget spent its 3 s to the microsecond and the link wait
 returned in 36 us with the link up. A clock that meters a 3.0000 s budget
 that exactly is advancing, and a wait that answers in 36 us is neither the
 count path nor a frozen counter. Both explanations for a ten-minute stall
 are DISFAVOURED here on that board, and the 14 minutes sitting 9 spent were
 spent somewhere after this, in `e1000-init`'s own bring-up rather than in
 this loop.

 The reachability question is separate and the answer is still yes: nothing in
 the diag context gates the count path, because `hpet-ticks-per-second` is a
 bare `peek-32` of a hardcoded base with no validation, so a board whose HPET
 period register reads zero takes it. What the budgets settle is that THIS
 board did not.

 THE CLOCK IS READ ONCE PER BATCH, not once per iteration, which is the pattern
 `e1000-await-tx-clocked` already uses in this chapter and for the same reason:
 a STATUS read is the cheap thing this loop is made of and an HPET read is not,
 so checking the clock every pass would measure the clock. A batch of 4096 is
 the granularity of the deadline and is far finer than a link needs.

 5000 ms is a BUDGET, not a datasheet figure. A gigabit link that is going to
 come up does so in a few seconds; five is generous enough not to cut off a slow
 switch and short enough that a part which never links fails in single-digit
 seconds instead of never. Worst case init is now that plus the 3 s aneg budget.

  e1000-link-window-ms : Integer = 5000
  e1000-link-batch : Integer = 4096
  e1000-link-batch-fuel : Integer = 100000

  e1000-link-ticks : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-link-ticks (rate) = (rate * e1000-link-window-ms) / 1000

  e1000-link-wait : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-link-wait (mmio) =
   let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-await-link mmio 0
   else e1000-await-link-clocked mmio hpet-ticks (e1000-link-ticks rate) e1000-link-batch-fuel

  e1000-await-link-batch : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-link-batch (mmio) (i) =
   if i <= 0 then 0
   else if bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status) e1000-status-lu /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-link-batch mmio (i - 1)

  e1000-await-link-clocked : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-link-clocked (mmio) (start) (want) (fuel) =
   if e1000-await-link-batch mmio e1000-link-batch == 1 then 1
   else if fuel <= 0 then 0
   else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then 0
   else e1000-await-link-clocked mmio start want (fuel - 1)

 THE COUNT SURVIVES as the no-HPET path, exactly as the aneg fuel does. With no
 usable clock the count is the only bound there is.

  e1000-await-link : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-link (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-link-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status) e1000-status-lu /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-link mmio (i + 1)

  e1000-has-link : E1000Device -> Boolean
  e1000-has-link (d) =
   if (d.e-present) then bit-and (e1000-read (d.e-mmio) e1000-reg-status) e1000-status-lu /= 0
   else False

Section: Station Address

 RAL and RAH are loaded from the part's own NVM before the driver runs,
 and RAH bit 31 is AV, address valid. A part whose AV bit is clear has
 not been given an address, and this driver reports that rather than
 inventing one: a fabricated MAC would let every later stage look
 healthy while the network refused to answer it.

  e1000-rah-av : Integer = #80000000

  e1000-mac-present : Integer -> Boolean
  e1000-mac-present (mmio) = bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rah) e1000-rah-av /= 0

  e1000-read-mac : Integer -> List Integer
  e1000-read-mac (mmio) =
   let lo = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ral
   in let hi = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-rah
   in [bit-and lo #FF, bit-and (bit-shru lo 8) #FF,
       bit-and (bit-shru lo 16) #FF, bit-and (bit-shru lo 24) #FF,
       bit-and hi #FF, bit-and (bit-shru hi 8) #FF]

Section: Receive Ring

 Each descriptor is given a buffer address and cleared status. The tail
 is set one behind the head, which is what hands every descriptor in the
 ring to the hardware: the region between head and tail is the driver's,
 and everything else is the device's to fill.

  e1000-setup-rx : E1000Device -> Integer
  e1000-setup-rx (d) =
   let mmio = d.e-mmio
   in let built = e1000-build-rx-descs d 0
   in let bl = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdbal (bit-and (d.e-rx-ring) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let bh = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdbah (bit-shru (d.e-rx-ring) 32)
   in let ln = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdlen (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size)
   in let h = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdh 0
   in let t = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdt (e1000-rx-count - 1)
   in let cur = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0 0
   in let promisc = bit-or e1000-rctl-upe e1000-rctl-mpe
   in let flags = bit-or e1000-rctl-secrc promisc
   in let rctl = bit-or e1000-rctl-en (bit-or e1000-rctl-bam flags)
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rctl rctl

  e1000-build-rx-descs : E1000Device, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-build-rx-descs (d) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-rx-count then 0
   else let desc = (d.e-rx-ring) + i * e1000-desc-size
   in let buf = (d.e-rx-bufs) + i * e1000-buf-size
   in let lo = poke-32 desc 0 (bit-and buf #FFFFFFFF)
   in let hi = poke-32 (desc + 4) 0 (bit-shru buf 32)
   in let mid = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 0
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in e1000-build-rx-descs d (i + 1)

Section: Transmit Ring

  e1000-setup-tx : E1000Device -> Integer
  e1000-setup-tx (d) =
   let mmio = d.e-mmio
   in let cleared = e1000-zero-words (d.e-tx-ring) (e1000-tx-count * 4) 0
   in let bl = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdbal (bit-and (d.e-tx-ring) #FFFFFFFF)
   in let bh = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdbah (bit-shru (d.e-tx-ring) 32)
   in let ln = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdlen (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size)
   in let h = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdh 0
   in let t = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tdt 0
   in let cur = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0 0
   in let g = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tipg e1000-tipg-default
   in let coll = bit-or e1000-tctl-ct e1000-tctl-cold
   in let tctl = bit-or e1000-tctl-en (bit-or e1000-tctl-psp coll)
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tctl tctl

Section: Multicast Table

 The multicast table array is 128 entries of 32 bits and its reset state
 is undefined, so it is cleared before receive is enabled.

  e1000-clear-mta : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-clear-mta (mmio) (i) =
   if i >= 128 then 0
   else let w = e1000-write mmio (e1000-reg-mta + i * 4) 0
   in e1000-clear-mta mmio (i + 1)

Section: Bring-up

  e1000-absent : E1000Device
  e1000-absent =
   E1000Device { e-mmio = 0, e-rx-ring = 0, e-tx-ring = 0, e-rx-bufs = 0,
                 e-tx-bufs = 0, e-ctrl-blk = 0, e-present = False,
                 e-mac = [], e-mac-valid = False, e-pch = 0, e-ulp = 0 }

Section: The PCH Layer

 8086:15b8 is Ethernet Connection (2) I219-V, and on that family the MAC lives
 in the PCH while the I219 is essentially the PHY. The 8254x parts this driver
 was written against are a combined MAC and PHY on a card, and the difference
 is not cosmetic: the PCH family has a documented and non-optional extra
 initialisation layer that an 8254x driver does not perform at all.
 `docs/Designs/Active/OS/I219IsNotAnE1000.md` has the table of eight and the
 campaign rules.

 K1 IS FIRST BECAUSE IT HAS A NAMED FAILURE MODE. With K1 enabled at 1 Gbps
 the MAC stalls, and the signature is exactly what five sittings photographed:
 link up, every register answering, nothing moving in either direction. Sitting 8
 then read the control on the board and found K1 enabled with the disable bit
 clear, which is that condition.

  e1000-dev-i219-v : Integer = #15B8

  e1000-pch-part : Integer -> Boolean
  e1000-pch-part (dev-id) = dev-id == e1000-dev-i219-v

 REQUIREMENT 2: THE MDIO/NVM SEMAPHORE. Cited in two halves with different
 strengths, and the difference is the point.

 CITED, 82583V section 4.5.2 and 9.2.2.15: `EXTCNF_CTRL` is at `0x00F00`, with
 MDIO/NVM SW Ownership at bit 5, MDIO/NVM HW Ownership at bit 6 and MDIO MNG
 Ownership at bit 7. A request is registered by writing 1b to your own bit.
 "The requesting agent is granted access when the same bit is read as 1b
 (access is not granted as long as the bit is 0b)." At most one bit is 1b at
 any time. The owner writes 0b when done. SW and HW ownership clear on reset;
 MNG clears only on LAN_PWR_GOOD or when firmware clears it.

 NOT CITED FOR THIS PART, and this is the half to be careful with. The same
 section says the mechanism "does not block software accesses to MDIO or the
 NVM, therefore programmers can enable software to use or ignore this process
 at will", and the bit table calls the register optional for the 82583V. That
 the PCH REFUSES MDIO without ownership is the vendor driver's behaviour, and
 the I219 datasheet mentions `EXTCNF_CTRL` only for the extended configuration
 area. The offset and the bits are family facts; the enforcement is the
 proposition the campaign exists to test.

 WHERE IT SITS, and it is the OPPOSITE end from K1 (red asked, 2026-08-21).
 SW ownership is cleared BY RESET, and the reset that clears it is the MAC
 reset in `e1000-reset`, not the PHY soft reset. So the semaphore is taken
 AFTER `e1000-reset` and before the first MDIO access, and held across the PHY
 work. K1 lives in a PHY register and is restored to its NVM value by the PHY
 soft reset, so it must be written AFTER `e1000-phy-bring-up`. Two resets, two
 registers, two placements, and each step in the other's position is silently
 undone by a reset that is not looking at it.

  e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl : Integer = #00F00
  e1000-extcnf-sw-own : Integer = #0020
  e1000-extcnf-hw-own : Integer = #0040
  e1000-extcnf-mng-own : Integer = #0080

 The grant is READ BACK and never inferred from the write, which is the whole
 of 4.5.2: the write either takes the bit or leaves it clear and never reports
 which. Fuel rather than a clock, because this runs before the HPET is known
 good and a bring-up that needs a clock to bring up is a dependency loop.

 THE REQUEST CARRIES ONLY OUR OWN BIT. 4.5.2 gives each agent one bit, says
 at most one is 1b at any time, and says the owner writes 0b when done, so a
 write that asserts MNG or HW is a request registered on another agent's
 behalf. Until 2026-08-24 this loop was `bit-or cur e1000-extcnf-sw-own` with
 no mask, which put firmware's MNG bit back as 1b every time the board held it
 (`extcnf 0x00F00=002c0089`, sittings 8 and 9). Measured under the bed's EXTCNF
 census with firmware holding MNG: 6,002 writes, 6,002 of them asserting a bit
 we do not own. Not 2,000, because the acquire runs from three call sites; the
 count registered when the defect was found was one site's.

 THE OWNERSHIP BITS ARE MASKED OFF AND THE REST IS PRESERVED, which is a
 narrower change than "write only bit 5" and deliberately so. The I219
 datasheet documents this register for the extended configuration area, so
 writing a bare `#0020` would zero the ext-config fields: that trades a
 measured defect for an unmeasured one on the one part of the register the
 part's own datasheet is about. The mask fixes what was proven wrong and
 leaves untouched what was not.

 A RELEASE WRITES 0b TO OUR BIT AND CANNOT DISTURB FIRMWARE'S. The same
 section says the MNG bit clears only on LAN_PWR_GOOD or by firmware, so a
 software 0 there is ignored by the part rather than being a release on
 firmware's behalf.

  e1000-extcnf-own-mask : Integer = #00E0

 THE COUNT IS BOUNDED WELL BELOW THE OLD ONE AND EACH POLL PAUSES. 2,000
 immediate retries is roughly 4,000 back-to-back MMIO transactions on the same
 PCH the xHCI is on, which is a burst rather than a wait. 64 polls at a 100 us
 window is 6.4 ms of patience for about 128 transactions. There is no timeout
 for this semaphore in either datasheet we hold, so these are OUR numbers and
 not cited ones. The no-clock fallback is a short spin rather than a long one
 for the same reason the count came down: with no clock every unit of waiting
 is itself MMIO, so a generous fallback would reintroduce the burst it
 replaces. Worst case with no clock is 64 * (2 + 32) = 2,176 transactions,
 still below the 4,000 this replaces.

  e1000-swflag-fuel : Integer = 64
  e1000-swflag-window-us : Integer = 100
  e1000-swflag-spin-fuel : Integer = 32

  e1000-swflag-held : Integer -> Boolean
  e1000-swflag-held (mmio) =
   bit-and (e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl) e1000-extcnf-sw-own /= 0

  e1000-swflag-request : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-swflag-request (mmio) =
   let cur = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl
   in let keep = bit-and cur (bit-not e1000-extcnf-own-mask)
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl (bit-or keep e1000-extcnf-sw-own)

  e1000-swflag-pause : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-swflag-pause (mmio) =
   let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-settle-spin mmio e1000-swflag-spin-fuel
   else e1000-await-ticks hpet-ticks ((rate * e1000-swflag-window-us) / 1000000) 0

  e1000-swflag-acquire : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-swflag-acquire (mmio) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then 0
   else let w = e1000-swflag-request mmio
   in if e1000-swflag-held mmio then 1
   else let d = e1000-swflag-pause mmio
   in e1000-swflag-acquire mmio (fuel - 1)

  e1000-swflag-release : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-swflag-release (mmio) =
   let cur = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-extcnf-ctrl (bit-and cur (bit-not e1000-extcnf-own-mask))

 IT SHIPPED OFF UNTIL 2026-08-21, and the pair that earned the flip is reek's
 `-i219-swflag` (MDIO refused without ownership) and `-i219-mng-holds`
 (firmware holding MNG, so a correct protocol is still refused), landed at
 main 18356. What turned it on is the coupling stated below, not either arm.

 A FAILED ACQUISITION PROCEEDS (red's ruling, 2026-08-21), and the board is
 what decided it. Sitting 8 read `mdio-gate open=n` with the reads working, so
 this part does NOT refuse an unowned caller: the enforcement half that the
 vendor driver's abort exists for is absent here, and the datasheet agrees that
 the mechanism "does not block software accesses". What remains is the ME race,
 which a bounded acquire narrows and an abort would not narrow at all -- it
 would turn a race into a refusal to bring the NIC up. So the acquire is
 fuel-bounded, its answer is reported, and the work happens either way.

  e1000-pch-swflag-required : Boolean = True

 IT IS ON BECAUSE K1 IS ON, and that coupling is the reason the flip is not
 optional (red, 2026-08-21). `e1000-pch-k1-required` shipping True means the
 driver now WRITES a PHY register during bring-up, and until this constant
 followed it that write went out with SW ownership clear: the exact unguarded
 MDIO access 4.5.2 exists to arbitrate. Two steps that were independent while
 both were off become one the moment the first is on.

 THE K1 WRITE IS GUARDED SEPARATELY, and it has to be. The semaphore taken in
 `e1000-init-after-reset` is released when bring-up finishes, and
 `e1000-pch-prepare` runs AFTER `e1000-init-at` returns, so the K1 step is
 outside that window by construction. `e1000-k1-configure-guarded` takes its
 own. Two windows rather than one long hold, because holding across the whole
 of bring-up would starve the ME for the duration of a link wait.

 THE CITED CONTROL, and it is the one this driver uses (red's ruling 18445).
 I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.5.2, PHY address 01, page 770, register 17: bit 13
 `Giga_K1_disable`, "When set, the I219 does not enter K1 while link speed at
 1000 Mb/s"; bit 14 `K1 enable`. Both RW, both default 0b. Disabling at 1 Gbps
 SETS bit 13 and leaves bit 14 alone, because the two describe different
 things: 14 is whether K1 exists at all, 13 is whether it may be entered at
 gigabit.

 The datasheet attaches a footnote to bit 14 that this driver does not yet act
 on: in SMBus mode the bit is cleared, and the register must be reconfigured
 after switching back to PCIe. That ties this step to the SMBus row of the
 eight rather than leaving it independent.

  e1000-phy-pcie-pm-page : Integer = 770
  e1000-phy-pcie-pm-reg : Integer = 17
  e1000-k1-giga-disable : Integer = #2000
  e1000-k1-enable-bit : Integer = #4000

 THIS RUNS AFTER THE PHY RESET, NEVER BEFORE. A PHY reset returns 770.17 to
 its NVM value, so a K1 configured ahead of `e1000-init-at` is wiped by the
 BMCR reset inside `e1000-phy-bring-up` and the part comes up in exactly the
 state the step was meant to leave. That is why `e1000-init` calls this layer
 after bring-up rather than before it.

 It answers the register READ BACK rather than the value written, so a caller
 can tell a bit that stuck from one that did not.

 ULP SPLITS IN TWO AND ONLY ONE HALF IS WRITABLE FROM WHAT WE HOLD. The ULP
 EXIT sequence is unspecified in our documents, which reek established on
 2026-08-21 and which is why no bed arm for it can exist; that half is not
 attempted here and must not be guessed at. ENTRY is a different question and
 it is fully cited: I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.7.1 maps ULP Configuration 1,
 PHY page 779 register 16, field by field. STICKY_ULP bit 4 is "Enter ULP on
 Link disconnect"; EN_ULP_LANPHYPC bit 10 is "Enable ULP on LAN disable
 (LANPHYPC)". Those two are what put the part into a state whose exit we
 cannot specify, so clearing them is the whole of "forcibly disabled" that
 this driver can honestly claim.

 WHAT THE BOARD ALREADY SAYS. Sittings 8 and 9 both banked
 `ulp 779.16=0800 ulp-ind=n path=OK`: bit 2 clear, so the part is not
 indicating a power-up from ULP, and bits 4 and 10 clear, so neither entry
 condition is armed. The write below is therefore a NO-OP on this board, which
 is the safest possible thing to ship into a bring-up whose hang is not yet
 named -- and 0x0800 is bit 11, inside the 13:11 the datasheet marks Reserved,
 so the one bit the part does set here is not one this step touches.

 The step is read-modify-write for the reason K1 is: the register holds
 firmware and NVM configuration, and the bits we are not addressing must
 survive.

  e1000-ulp-page : Integer = 779
  e1000-ulp-reg : Integer = 16
  e1000-ulp-sticky : Integer = #0010
  e1000-ulp-lanphypc : Integer = #0400
  e1000-ulp-entry-bits : Integer = #0410

  e1000-ulp-disable : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-ulp-disable (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio e1000-ulp-page
   in let v0 = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-ulp-reg
   in if v0 < 0 then (let restore0 = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0 in 0 - 1)
   else let v1 = bit-and v0 (bit-not e1000-ulp-entry-bits)
   in let w = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-ulp-reg v1
   in let back = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-ulp-reg
   in let restore = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in back

 IT SHIPS OFF on the same terms as K1, the semaphore and the LCD reload: the
 write is cited but nothing has flown it, and the bed cannot yet answer it at
 all because codex-vm models page 770 and not this register -- which the
 `pch` stage's own neighbour control already reports as `ulp 779.16=ERR` with
 `neighbour-id1=0154`. The fly gate wants a skipping arm red against a model
 that implements the step; that model does not exist yet.

  e1000-pch-ulp-required : Boolean = False

  e1000-ulp-step : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-ulp-step (mmio) (dev-id) =
   if e1000-pch-ulp-required == False then 0
   else if e1000-pch-part dev-id == False then 0
   else let v = e1000-ulp-disable mmio
   in if v < 0 then (0 - 1)
   else if bit-and v e1000-ulp-entry-bits == 0 then 1
   else 2

  e1000-k1-configure : Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  e1000-k1-configure (mmio) (enable) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-page
   in let v0 = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-reg
   in let v1 = if enable then bit-and v0 (bit-not e1000-k1-giga-disable) else bit-or v0 e1000-k1-giga-disable
   in let w = e1000-phy-write mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-reg v1
   in let back = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-pcie-pm-reg
   in let restore = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in back

 THE OMISSION IS A KNOB ON PURPOSE, and the fly gate is what it is for
 (`I219IsNotAnE1000.md`, "THE FLY GATE"). A bed that only ever agrees is worth
 nothing, so the pair that proves both halves is one build reaching the step
 and one skipping it: the skipping arm must come back RED against a model that
 implements the requirement, and the reaching arm GREEN on the same bed and
 the same image. A runtime knob rather than a second compile because the arms
 already carry DIAG.CFG and a second image costs a compile and a hash nobody
 checks.

 It is not a foot-gun left lying about: `e1000-pch-prepare` reports the state
 it left, and omitting the step on a part that needs it is exactly the failure
 the row is there to name.

 THIS BLOCK SAID "EVERY CALLER PAINTS IT" AND THAT WAS FALSE FOR AS LONG AS IT
 STOOD. `e1000-init` bound the answer to `pch` and read it nowhere, and it is
 the only production caller, so the reported state reached nothing at all. The
 answer now lands on the device as `e-pch`, which is what makes the sentence
 above true rather than aspirational. Sitting 9 is what exposed it: the K1
 write is the first thing bring-up does that `nicinit` does not, and no row
 could say what it did.

 THIS IS THE OMISSION POINT. `e1000-pch-k1-required` is the one line either
 build changes, and it is a named constant rather than an inline literal so
 that the fly gate's two builds differ by a value and not by an edit somebody
 has to describe in prose.

 IT SHIPPED OFF UNTIL 2026-08-21, and that was a decision rather than a
 placeholder. The layer wrote registers on a part nothing had modelled, so
 until the fly gate's pair was green the honest default was the one that
 touches nothing: the board IS a `15b8` part, and a `True` on main would have
 put an unproven write into every image composed from it, including sitting 7,
 which existed to measure the board rather than to change it.

 IT IS ON SINCE 2026-08-21, and the thing that turned it on is a BOARD READING
 rather than a bed result. Sitting 8 read `770.17 = 0xd104` on the ASUS: K1
 enabled, `Giga_K1_disable` clear. That is the condition this step exists for,
 measured on the part, so the layer is NEEDED and not merely conforming, which
 is the distinction red held the flip open for.

 The bed pair is green either way and says the smaller thing:
 `codex/test/e1000-pch-k1` transmits nothing with K1 at its NVM setting,
 transmits after the step, and re-runs the PHY reset to show the requirement
 coming back. Both arms produce their own omission rather than borrowing this
 constant, which is what let the default move without either pair losing a
 side.

 THE BOARD'S VALUE IS NOT THE BED'S, and the step is written for the board.
 The model powers up at `0x4000`; the ASUS reads `0xd104`, five bits apart and
 three of them different from the datasheet's own reset defaults, because that
 register holds firmware and NVM configuration on a real part. This is why
 `e1000-k1-configure` READ-MODIFY-WRITES rather than writing a constant: the
 board becomes `0xf104` and the five firmware bits survive.

  e1000-pch-k1-required : Boolean = True

 The four answers are distinct on purpose: 2 and 3 both mean the step RAN, and
 they differ on whether the bit stuck, which is the one thing a caller cannot
 recover afterwards.

 THE ACQUIRE RESULT WAS BOUND AND NEVER READ until 2026-08-21, and sitting 9
 is what made that cost something. The board reads `extcnf 0x00F00=002c0089`,
 bit 7 set: firmware holds MNG ownership, so `e1000-swflag-acquire` cannot
 succeed there and at most one of the two bits can be 1b. The step then wrote
 K1 anyway, which is red's standing ruling and stays -- bounded acquire,
 PROCEED on failure -- but it wrote with nothing recording that it had gone in
 unguarded, so no row anywhere could say whether the write this campaign is
 about happened with ownership or without it.

 Proceeding is a POLICY and reporting is not optional, which is why the answer
 carries both halves rather than a single number that cannot tell them apart.

  K1Step = record {
    k1-value : Integer,
    k1-owned : Boolean
  }

  e1000-k1-configure-guarded : Integer -> K1Step
  e1000-k1-configure-guarded (mmio) =
   if e1000-pch-swflag-required == False then K1Step { k1-value = e1000-k1-configure mmio False, k1-owned = True }
   else let got = e1000-swflag-acquire mmio e1000-swflag-fuel
   in let v = e1000-k1-configure mmio False
   in let rel = e1000-swflag-release mmio
   in K1Step { k1-value = v, k1-owned = got /= 0 }

 The answers keep two independent questions apart, ownership and outcome:

     0  not an I219, the step did not run
     1  not asked for
     2  owned, the bit did not stick
     3  owned, the bit stuck
     4  unowned, the bit did not stick
     5  unowned, the bit stuck
     6  owned, MDIO did not answer
     7  unowned, MDIO did not answer

 A caller cannot recover either half afterwards: ownership is gone by the time
 anything reads back, and the bit can be cleared by the next PHY reset.

 THE UNREADABLE CASE NEEDS ITS OWN PAIR AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS DID NOT
 HAVE IT, which `codex/test/e1000-swflag-refused` caught on its first run.
 `e1000-k1-configure` answers -1 when MDIO is refused, and -1 has every bit
 set, so testing the disable bit against it reports STUCK for a register that
 said nothing at all. The board is exactly that case: `-i219-mng-holds` in the
 bed and `mdio-gate 769.16=2180 open=n` on the ASUS both answer 7.

  e1000-pch-prepare : Integer, Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  e1000-pch-prepare (mmio) (dev-id) (do-k1) =
   if e1000-pch-part dev-id == False then 0
   else if do-k1 == False then 1
   else let step = e1000-k1-configure-guarded mmio
   in if step.k1-value < 0 then (if step.k1-owned then 6 else 7)
   else let stuck = bit-and (step.k1-value) e1000-k1-giga-disable /= 0
   in if step.k1-owned then (if stuck then 3 else 2)
   else if stuck then 5
   else 4

 REQUIREMENT 3: THE LCD REGISTERS AFTER A PHY SOFT RESET.

 CITED, I219 rev 2.02 section 5.2 "Reset Operation", under PHY Soft Reset: "A
 PHY reset caused by writing to bit 15 in MDIO register 0. Setting the bit
 resets the PHY, but does not reset non-PHY parts. The PHY registers are
 reset, but other I219 registers are not." Then the obligation, in the same
 note: "The integrated LAN controller configures the LCD registers."

 WHICH RESET CLEARS IT, because that is the whole of where it sits (red asked
 for this explicitly, and it is the two-boundary rule the semaphore and K1
 already sit on opposite ends of). The LCD registers are PHY registers, so
 THE PHY SOFT RESET clears them and the MAC reset does not. This is therefore
 the general obligation behind K1's own placement rather than a second rule:
 K1 lives at 770.17, which is one of the registers this sentence is about.

 `e1000-init` PAYS IT BY CONSTRUCTION and nothing says so at either site.
 `e1000-pch-prepare` runs after `e1000-init-at` returns, which is after the
 BMCR reset inside `e1000-phy-bring-up`, so the shipped bring-up ends with the
 LCD registers configured. Reorder those two lines and the requirement is
 silently unmet, which is what `codex/test/e1000-lcd-reload` now watches.

 THE ASDE PATH DOES NOT PAY IT. `na-phy-kick` writes BMCR bit 15 and nothing
 reconfigures behind it, so an ASDE arm hands whatever runs next a part whose
 K1 is back at its NVM value. That is measured rather than argued: rows 1 and
 3 of the arm are that path, 770.17 at 16384 and the send refused.

  e1000-pch-lcd-reload-required : Boolean = False

 IT SHIPS OFF on red's terms, the same terms K1 and the semaphore shipped on:
 the pair is green in the bed and nothing has flown it. Flipping it is not
 enough on its own, and this is the part to read before flipping: the flight
 path reaches the reset through `dasd-run-part` and its siblings, which carry
 an MMIO base and not a device, so no caller there can supply the `dev-id`
 this gate needs. The flip and that one-parameter thread are one change.

  e1000-lcd-reload : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-lcd-reload (mmio) (dev-id) =
   if e1000-pch-lcd-reload-required == False then 0
   else e1000-pch-prepare mmio dev-id e1000-pch-k1-required

  e1000-init : PciDevice -> E1000Device
  e1000-init (pci-dev) =
   let enabled = pci-enable-device pci-dev
   in let bar = pci-parse-bar (pci-dev.pci-bus) (pci-dev.pci-dev) (pci-dev.pci-func) 0
   in if e1000-bar-verdict bar /= e1000-bar-ok then e1000-absent
   else let d = e1000-init-at (bar.bar-base)
   in let ulp = e1000-ulp-step (bar.bar-base) (pci-dev.pci-device-id)
   in let pch = e1000-pch-prepare (bar.bar-base) (pci-dev.pci-device-id) e1000-pch-k1-required
   in __record-set (__record-set d "e-pch" pch) "e-ulp" ulp

  e1000-init-at : Integer -> E1000Device
  e1000-init-at (mmio) =
   if e1000-reset mmio == 0 then e1000-absent
   else e1000-init-after-reset (mmio)

 A device this driver could not reset arrives in whatever state firmware
 and the manageability engine left it, and on the I219 that is the ONLY
 state available: CTRL is read-only there, so the RST write in e1000-reset
 is discarded and e1000-await-reset answers settled on its first read. The
 receiver may still be running against a ring belonging to somebody else,
 and the ring registers must not be reprogrammed underneath a live one.

 Disabling receive and transmit is therefore done unconditionally rather
 than only when the reset failed. On a part that did reset these registers
 are already zero and the writes cost two MMIO cycles; on a part that did
 not, they are the only thing standing between setup-rx and a receive path
 that reports healthy and never receives. Measured 2026-08-13 under
 -e1000-ctrl-ro -e1000-preconfigured: present yes, link up yes, mac valid
 yes, send accepted 1, and recv happened NO.

  e1000-quiesce-ms : Integer = 10

  e1000-quiesce : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-quiesce (mmio) =
   let r = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rctl 0
   in let t = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-tctl 0
   in let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-settle-spin mmio e1000-settle-spin-fuel
   else e1000-await-ticks hpet-ticks ((rate * e1000-quiesce-ms) / 1000) 0

  e1000-init-after-reset : Integer -> E1000Device
  e1000-init-after-reset (mmio) =
   let quiet = e1000-quiesce mmio
   in let rx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let tx-ring = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-desc-size) 16
   in let rx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-rx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let tx-bufs = e1000-alloc-aligned (e1000-tx-count * e1000-buf-size) 16
   in let ctrl-blk = e1000-alloc-aligned e1000-cb-size 8
   in let cb-clear = e1000-zero-words ctrl-blk (e1000-cb-size / 4) 0
   in let mta = e1000-clear-mta mmio 0
   in let mac-ok = e1000-mac-present mmio
   in let mac = if mac-ok then e1000-read-mac mmio else []
   in let d = E1000Device { e-mmio = mmio, e-rx-ring = rx-ring,
                            e-tx-ring = tx-ring, e-rx-bufs = rx-bufs,
                            e-tx-bufs = tx-bufs, e-ctrl-blk = ctrl-blk,
                            e-present = True, e-mac = mac,
                            e-mac-valid = mac-ok, e-pch = 0, e-ulp = 0 }
   in let rx = e1000-setup-rx d
   in let tx = e1000-setup-tx d
   in let sem = if e1000-pch-swflag-required then e1000-swflag-acquire mmio e1000-swflag-fuel else 1
   in let link = e1000-link-up mmio
   in let rel = if e1000-pch-swflag-required then e1000-swflag-release mmio else 0
   in d

Section: Receive

  E1000RecvResult = record {
    r-frame : List Integer,
    r-has-frame : Boolean
  }

  e1000-no-frame : E1000RecvResult
  e1000-no-frame = E1000RecvResult { r-frame = [], r-has-frame = False }

  e1000-poll-frame : E1000Device -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-poll-frame (d) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then e1000-no-frame
   else e1000-poll-raw (d.e-mmio) (d.e-rx-ring) (d.e-rx-bufs) (d.e-ctrl-blk)

  e1000-poll-raw : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-poll-raw (mmio) (rx-ring) (rx-bufs) (ctrl-blk) =
   if mmio == 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else let idx = bit-and (peek-32 (ctrl-blk + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let desc = rx-ring + idx * e1000-desc-size
   in let status = peek-byte desc 12
   in if bit-and status e1000-rx-status-dd == 0 then e1000-no-frame
   else e1000-take-frame mmio rx-bufs ctrl-blk idx desc status

  e1000-take-frame : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> E1000RecvResult
  e1000-take-frame (mmio) (rx-bufs) (ctrl-blk) (idx) (desc) (status) =
   let len = bit-or (peek-byte desc 8) (bit-shl (peek-byte desc 9) 8)
   in let buf = rx-bufs + idx * e1000-buf-size
   in let capped = if len > e1000-max-frame then e1000-max-frame else len
   in let whole = bit-and status e1000-rx-status-eop /= 0
   in let frame = if whole then e1000-read-bytes buf 0 capped [] else []
   in let recycled = e1000-recycle-rx mmio ctrl-blk idx desc
   in E1000RecvResult { r-frame = frame, r-has-frame = whole }

  e1000-recycle-rx : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-recycle-rx (mmio) (ctrl-blk) (idx) (desc) =
   let cleared = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 0
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in let next = int-mod (idx + 1) e1000-rx-count
   in let saved = poke-32 (ctrl-blk + e1000-cb-rx-next) 0 next
   in e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-rdt idx

  e1000-read-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  e1000-read-bytes (base) (i) (len) (acc) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else e1000-read-bytes base (i + 1) len (list-push acc (peek-byte base i))

Section: Transmit

  e1000-send-frame : E1000Device, List Integer -> Integer
  e1000-send-frame (d) (frame) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then 0
   else let len = list-length frame
   in if len <= 0 then 0
   else if len > e1000-max-frame then 0
   else e1000-send-at d frame len

  e1000-send-at : E1000Device, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-send-at (d) (frame) (len) =
   let idx = bit-and (peek-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in let desc = (d.e-tx-ring) + idx * e1000-desc-size
   in let buf = (d.e-tx-bufs) + idx * e1000-buf-size
   in let staged = e1000-write-bytes buf frame 0 len
   in let lo = poke-32 desc 0 (bit-and buf #FFFFFFFF)
   in let hi = poke-32 (desc + 4) 0 (bit-shru buf 32)
   in let mid = poke-32 (desc + 8) 0 (bit-or (bit-and len #FFFF) (bit-shl e1000-tx-cmd-all 24))
   in let top = poke-32 (desc + 12) 0 0
   in let next = int-mod (idx + 1) e1000-tx-count
   in let saved = poke-32 ((d.e-ctrl-blk) + e1000-cb-tx-next) 0 next
   in let kick = e1000-write (d.e-mmio) e1000-reg-tdt next
   in e1000-await-tx desc

 The command byte sits at bit 24 of the third descriptor word, above
 the sixteen-bit length at bit 0 and the checksum-offset byte at bit 16
 which this driver leaves zero.

  e1000-tx-cmd-all : Integer =
   bit-or e1000-tx-cmd-eop (bit-or e1000-tx-cmd-ifcs e1000-tx-cmd-rs)

  e1000-tx-fuel : Integer = 1000000

 The transmit wait is bounded by the CLOCK, and the fuel below it is only a
 backstop for a machine with no usable HPET. It was a bare spin count until
 2026-08-14, and the count was a duration nobody had worked out.

 Measured on this box: one million iterations of exactly this loop shape
 cost 605 microseconds, because a descriptor status byte is a RAM read that
 the card DMAd into memory, with no register access anywhere in the loop.
 So `e1000-tx-fuel` was a 605 microsecond budget. A 1500-byte frame takes 12
 microseconds on the wire at 1 Gb/s, 120 at 100 Mb/s and **1200 at 10 Mb/s**,
 so the budget expired BEFORE the frame could physically leave, and
 `e1000-send-frame` returns this value directly: on a 10 Mb/s link every send
 would have reported failure while the frame went out anyway. The part
 negotiated 1000 Mb/s on the ASUS, which is the only reason this never
 showed.

 The clock is read once per BATCH, not once per iteration. `hpet-ticks` is
 an MMIO read and this loop's whole character is that it does not make one;
 checking it per iteration would slow the loop by about three orders of
 magnitude and measure the clock rather than the descriptor. A batch of 4096
 is about 2.5 microseconds, which is the granularity of the deadline and is
 far finer than any link needs.

  e1000-tx-window-ms : Integer = 20
  e1000-tx-batch : Integer = 4096
  e1000-tx-batch-fuel : Integer = 100000

  e1000-await-tx-batch : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-tx-batch (desc) (i) =
   if i <= 0 then 0
   else if bit-and (peek-byte desc 12) e1000-tx-status-dd /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-tx-batch desc (i - 1)

  e1000-await-tx-clocked : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-tx-clocked (desc) (start) (want) (fuel) =
   if e1000-await-tx-batch desc e1000-tx-batch == 1 then 1
   else if fuel <= 0 then 0
   else if (hpet-ticks - start) >= want then 0
   else e1000-await-tx-clocked desc start want (fuel - 1)

  e1000-await-tx-spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-tx-spin (desc) (i) =
   if i >= e1000-tx-fuel then 0
   else if bit-and (peek-byte desc 12) e1000-tx-status-dd /= 0 then 1
   else e1000-await-tx-spin desc (i + 1)

  e1000-await-tx : Integer -> Integer
  e1000-await-tx (desc) =
   let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then e1000-await-tx-spin desc 0
   else e1000-await-tx-clocked desc hpet-ticks ((rate * e1000-tx-window-ms) / 1000) e1000-tx-batch-fuel

  e1000-write-bytes : Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  e1000-write-bytes (base) (bs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then 0
   else let w = poke-byte base i (list-at bs i)
   in e1000-write-bytes base bs (i + 1) len

Page 1
